// `@cfg(cond)` keeps a declaration iff `cond` holds, and the condition is ordinary
// annotation syntax -- a name, or a name applied to more of them. It used to be a string
// (`@cfg("not(windows)")`) that `expand` tokenised and parsed by hand; a condition now
// goes through the same parser as everything else, so an unbalanced one is a parse error
// with a span rather than a message pointing at the whole annotation.
//
// The conditions here are deliberately host-independent: the driver seeds the config with
// the host's OS and arch, so a test naming either would pass on one machine and drop the
// wrong branch on another. `nowhere` is a key no build sets, which is a *false* key rather
// than an error -- there is no registry of legal keys, and asking about one this build has
// never heard of is the normal case.

use std::io;

// Two declarations of the same name, one of which is dropped. This is the property that
// makes `@cfg` worth having: an omitted decl leaves no trace for name resolution, so this
// is not a redefinition -- it is one definition, chosen.
@cfg(nowhere)
fn platform() -> str { "the impossible branch" }

@cfg(not(nowhere))
fn platform() -> str { "the branch that survives" }

// Nesting, and both connectives. `all` and `any` fold from their identity, and an empty
// one is an error rather than a vacuous answer, so every condition here has an operand.
@cfg(all(not(nowhere), any(nowhere, not(nowhere))))
fn nested() -> str { "nested conditions hold" }

// A `@cfg` on a mod is decided before its body is walked, so annotations inside a dropped
// branch are never looked up -- which is what lets code written for another target use
// annotations this build does not have.
@cfg(nowhere)
mod dropped {
    @not_an_annotation
    fn never_seen() {}
}

@cfg(not(nowhere))
mod kept {
    fn hello() -> str { "from a kept mod" }
}

fn main() {
    io::println(platform());
    io::println(nested());
    io::println(kept::hello());
}
